From openbsd-alpha  Tue Nov 27 21:10:30 2001
From: Alexander Poquet <atpoquet () ucdavis ! edu>
Date: Tue, 27 Nov 2001 21:10:30 +0000
To: openbsd-alpha
Subject: AlphaBIOS to SRM firmware conversion on an AlphaPC 164LX 533MHz -- Help!
X-MARC-Message: https://marc.info/?l=openbsd-alpha&m=100689561528307

Hello all,

I recently acquired an AlphaPC 164LX 533MHz workstation which had been (god
forbid) running Windows NT, and as such was equipped with the Windows NT
accelerated AlphaBIOS firmware rather than the SRM firmware required to boot
our OS of choice.

I went to http://ftp.digital.com/pub/DEC/Alpha/firmware and followed the
instructions for my hardware, acquiring a file called lx164_v5_8.exe.  I
formatted a FAT floppy and renamed this file to 'fwupdate.exe' (as per the
instructions) and chose 'Update AlphaBIOS' from the AlphaBIOS menu.

It frobbed the floppy for a bit and then rebooted, and gave me this:
(the floppy is still in the drive)

---- BEGIN LOG ----
eb.ea.e9.e8.e7
Checking dka600.6.0.2000.0 for the option firmware files. . .
dka600.6.0.2000.0 has no media present or is disabled via the RUN/STOP switch
Checking dva0.0.0.0.0 for the option firmware files. . .
If you have a floppy containing option firmware,
Please insert it now and hit <return> when ready.
Option firmware not copied.


                ***** Loadable Firmware Update Utility *****
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
-
 Function    Description
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
-

 Display     Displays the system's configuration table.
 Exit        Done exit LFU (reset).
 List        Lists the device, revision, firmware name, and update revision.
 Readme      Lists important release information.
 Update      Replaces current firmware with loadable data image.
 Verify      Compares loadable and hardware images.
 ? or Help   Scrolls this function table.
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
-


UPD>
---- END LOG ----

If I try reseting the system (via the Exit command) without the floppy in
the drive I end up with a very similar situation (it says something a little
bit different but with similar meaning after checking dva0.0.0.0.0 for the
firmware files).

Now, the meat of the problem.

---- BEGIN LOG ----
UPD> update

Confirm update on:
fsb
fw
[Y/(N)]y
WARNING: updates may take several minutes to complete for each device.

                          DO NOT ABORT!

fsb             Updating to 3.1...  Verifying 3.1...  PASSED.

fw              open failure firmware filename 'nt_fw'

UPD>
---- END LOG ----

Now I'm taking an absolutely wild guess here, but does 'fsb' maybe stand for
'flash system bios' or something, and 'fw' for firmware?

If so, why is it looking for NT firmware (presumably the AlphaBIOS) which
obviously isn't on the disk I have in the drive, as I'm trying to install
SRM?

The verify command produces similar output, but without the
'Updating to 3.1...' bit.

I've looked about on the net to see if other people trying to install
unix like OSs on an AlphaPC have had similar problems, and descriptions
(but no solutions) of this problem on AlphaPC 164LX and AlphaPC 164SX boxes
abound.  It appears to be a reasonably common problem, but many folks have
managed to get {Open,Free,Net}BSD, Linux, Tru64 Unix, etc running on these
boxes (all of which require SRM firmware).

I'm hoping one of you has a solution and/or experience with this problem.
Is this list archived anywhere?  It ought to be, if it isn't; there's very
little documentation out there about getting OpenBSD running on the alpha
architecture and such an archive would be an invaluable resource to newbies
like me.  A FAQ or something would be great... maybe we could start
compiling one, informally?  It would be silly to have people with Alphas
decide to run competing OSs on their boxes just because they couldn't find
any documentation.  :)

Any help is greatly appreciated, I'd like to get the SRM working before my
OpenBSD 3.0 CDs get here!

Oh, by the way, the 'Display' command produces so much output about PCI and
SCSI devices that I can't get it all on screen.  I'd be happy to include it
if any of you know how to pipe it through a pager :)
